 "literal": "Good words having disregarded, mean words having spread / for rain water received unbaked clay pot like / mind not bearing flood having swum / difficult desire you attained, O heart! Very much / great indeed, alas, your struggle! Of high branch / young-possessing female monkey like / chest-to-fit having embraced, those who listen if you obtain.",
 "translation": "O heart! Disregarding good advice and filled with useless thoughts, you are like an unbaked clay pot receiving rain water, dissolving away. Swimming through a flood of passion that the mind cannot contain, you have set your desire on what is hard to attain. Indeed, your struggle is immense! If only you could find someone who would listen to your grief, embracing you closely to their chest, just like a female monkey on a high branch holding her young one.",
 "modern": "நல்ல அறிவுரைகளைப் புறக்கணித்து, வீணான சொற்கள் பரவ, பெய்கின்ற மழையை ஏற்கும் சுடாத மண்பாண்டம் கரைவது போல, நெஞ்சால் தாங்க முடியாத ஆசை வெள்ளத்தில் நீந்தி, அடைவதற்கு அரிய பொருளின் மேல் ஆசை கொண்டாய் என் நெஞ்சே! உயர்ந்த கிளையில் இருக்கும் குட்டியையுடைய பெண் குரங்கு தன் குட்டியை மார்போடு அணைத்துக் கொள்வது போல, உன் துன்பத்தைக் கேட்டு மார்போடு அணைத்துக் கொள்ளும் ஆட்களைப் பெற்றால், உன் போராட்டம் மிகவும் பெரியதுதான்!",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the hero, who is deeply in love and unable to reach his beloved. He addresses his own heart, which is suffering from intense desire. He compares his heart to an unbaked clay pot exposed to rain water, which quickly dissolves and collapses under the wetness. His heart is drowning in a flood of passion that it cannot contain. He notes that his heart's struggle is immense, and laments that there is no one to listen to his grief and comfort him. He uses the poignant image of a mother monkey on a high branch holding her baby tightly to her chest to describe the kind of intimate, protective comfort his heart desperately seeks but cannot find. This poem belongs to the Kuṟiñci tiṇai, where the high branches and monkeys are natural elements that mirror the height of his desire and the depth of his vulnerability.",
